# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <vx/mem.h>
#include <vx/err.h>

vx_arena *vx_arena_new(size_t reserve);
void vx_arena_free(vx_arena *a);
void *vx_push(vx_arena *a, size_t size, size_t align);
vx_mark vx_arena_mark(const vx_arena *a);
void vx_arena_pop(vx_arena *a, vx_mark m);
vx_status vx_arena_error(const vx_arena *a);
vx_arena *vx_scratch(vx_arena *const *conflicts, size_t n);

vx_pool *vx_pool_new(vx_arena *a, size_t slot_size, size_t max);
vx_id vx_pool_take(vx_pool *p);
void *vx_pool_get(const vx_pool *p, vx_id id);
void vx_pool_put(vx_pool *p, vx_id id);
vx_status vx_pool_error(const vx_pool *p);

vx_str vx_errstr(void);
```

# DESCRIPTION

An arena is the first of `libvx`'s three kinds of memory (09 §4.3, M6 step 6e4c1): memory given out in order and taken back all at once, for everything that lives as long as one piece of work.
`vx_arena_new` reserves <reserve> bytes, rounded up to a page, as one lazy VMO (vmo(2), ADR-0046): the address space is the arena's at once, and a page costs nothing until it is touched.
If it cannot, it returns the nil arena, on which every call does nothing and `vx_push` gives `nullptr`.
`vx_arena_free` gives the whole arena back.

`vx_push` returns <size> bytes aligned to <align>, a power of two up to 4096 (anything else is 16), and cleared to zeros; it makes no call to the kernel.
When the arena is full it returns `nullptr`, and the arena remembers it: `vx_arena_error` says `VX_ERR_NO_MEMORY` from then on, so a long piece of work may check once at its end.

`vx_arena_mark` notes how much is pushed, and `vx_arena_pop` takes everything pushed since back.

Each thread has two scratch arenas of 64 MiB, made when first asked for and given back when the thread ends.
`vx_scratch` returns one that is none of the <n> arenas in <conflicts>: a function passes the arenas its caller gave it, so its scratch never overlaps memory it is returning into.
It marks the scratch arena, uses it, and pops it before returning.

A pool is slots of <slot_size> bytes, at most <max> of them, pushed onto arena <a> at once.
`vx_pool_take` gives a cleared slot and its id, or 0 when all are taken.
An id is the slot's index and a generation: once `vx_pool_put` gives the slot back, `vx_pool_get` returns `nullptr` for the old id, so a stale handle finds nothing rather than someone else's object.

`vx_errstr` is the calling thread's last error, in words: why a call failed, past its `vx_status`.
A full arena or pool sets it, as the calls of later steps will, a server's error among them.

# EXAMPLES

Scratch space for a function that returns into its caller's arena:

```c
vx_str label(vx_arena *out, uint64_t id) {
  vx_arena *tmp = vx_scratch(&out, 1);
  vx_mark m = vx_arena_mark(tmp);
  vx_str name = lookup(tmp, id);  // working memory
  vx_str s = vx_fmt(out, "%.*s #%llu", VX_FMT(name), (unsigned long long)id);
  vx_arena_pop(tmp, m);
  return s;
}
```

